LEETCODE 84 — LARGEST RECTANGLE IN HISTOGRAM

CORE IDEA:

Each bar can be part of a rectangle that spans multiple consecutive bars.

Area = height × width

The rectangle does NOT have to use only 2 bars.

Example:

[2, 2, 2]

height = 2
width = 3

area = 2 × 3 = 6


STACK:

Use a Stack<Integer> to store INDEXES, not heights.

Stack:
[0, 1, 2]

To get the height:

h[stack.peek()]

Important:

stack.peek()      → gives the INDEX
h[stack.peek()]   → gives the HEIGHT


MAIN LOOP:

for(int i = 0; i < h.length; i++)


CASE 1:

if(stack.isEmpty()){
    stack.push(i);
}

If the stack is empty, simply push the current index.


CASE 2:

else if(h[stack.peek()] <= h[i]){
    stack.push(i);
}

If the current height is greater than or equal to
the height at the top of the stack:

→ push the current index.

This keeps the stack in increasing order of heights.


CASE 3:

else{

    while(!stack.isEmpty() && h[stack.peek()] > h[i]){

        int ht = h[stack.pop()];

        int w;

        if(stack.isEmpty()){
            w = i;
        }
        else{
            w = i - stack.peek() - 1;
        }

        int area = ht * w;

        if(area > ma){
            ma = area;
        }
    }

    stack.push(i);
}


WHY DO WE POP?

When a smaller height appears:

Example:

[5, 6, 2]

When 2 arrives:

6 > 2

So the rectangle with height 6 cannot continue past index 2.

Pop 6 and calculate its maximum possible rectangle.

Then:

5 > 2

Pop 5 and calculate its maximum possible rectangle.

The current smaller bar acts as the RIGHT boundary.


WIDTH CALCULATION:

After popping a height:

If the stack is empty:

w = i;

This means there is no smaller bar on the left.

Therefore, the rectangle can extend from index 0
up to index i - 1.

Example:

[5, 6, 7, 2]

When 2 arrives at i = 3:

5, 6, 7 are popped.

For height 5:

stack becomes empty.

width = i
      = 3

So:

area = 5 × 3
     = 15


IF STACK IS NOT EMPTY:

w = i - stack.peek() - 1;

Why?

stack.peek() = index of the previous smaller bar.

Current i = index of the next smaller bar.

The rectangle exists between these two smaller bars.

Example:

indexes:

0  1  2  3
2  5  6  2

For height 5:

previous smaller = index 0
next smaller = index 3

width:

3 - 0 - 1
= 2

So height 5 covers:

[5, 6]

area:

5 × 2 = 10


SECOND WHILE LOOP:

After the main for loop finishes, there may still be
indexes inside the stack.

Example:

[2, 4, 4, 4]

There is no smaller bar after the 4s.

So the main loop never gets a chance to calculate
their maximum areas.

Therefore:

while(!stack.isEmpty()){

    int ht = h[stack.pop()];

    int w;

    if(stack.isEmpty()){
        w = h.length;
    }
    else{
        w = h.length - stack.peek() - 1;
    }

    int area = ht * w;

    if(area > ma){
        ma = area;
    }
}


WHY h.length?

We have reached the END of the array.

So the end of the array acts like a smaller bar.

If the stack becomes empty:

→ there is no smaller bar on the left
→ rectangle can extend from index 0
→ width = h.length


IMPORTANT FORMULAS:

Area:

area = height × width


When stack becomes empty:

width = i


When stack is not empty:

width = i - stack.peek() - 1


At the end of the array:

width = h.length

or:

width = h.length - stack.peek() - 1


EXAMPLE:

h = [2,1,5,6,2,3]

When 2 at index 4 arrives:

6 is popped:

height = 6
width = 1
area = 6


5 is popped:

height = 5
width = 2
area = 10

So:

ma = 10


KEY CONCEPT:

The stack stores indexes of bars in increasing height order.

When a smaller bar arrives:

→ pop taller bars
→ calculate their maximum possible width
→ calculate area
→ continue

This allows us to find the largest rectangle efficiently.


KEY SYNTAXES:

Stack<Integer> stack = new Stack<>();

stack.push(i);
→ add index to stack

stack.pop();
→ remove and return top index

stack.peek();
→ see top index

stack.isEmpty();
→ check whether stack is empty

h[stack.peek()];
→ get the height of the bar whose index is at the top


TIME COMPLEXITY:

O(n)

Each index is pushed once and popped once.


SPACE COMPLEXITY:

O(n)

The stack can contain up to n indexes.
